> API and interpretation

Generation labels identify how the synthetic points were constructed, not
guaranteed threshold membership. The displayed final inlier mask is explicitly
rescored against the returned TLS coefficients. The all-point OLS baseline uses
the canonical native LM API, replacing report-local sums and normal equations.
Duplicate-index attempts count toward the requested trials but do not score a
model. Distinct tiny points are no longer discarded solely by a fixed
absolute1e-12 norm cutoff. Finite widened arithmetic improves range handling
without claiming exact floating geometric classification; unrepresentable
diagnostics throw. Threshold sensitivity on one fixture is descriptive, not
held-out hyperparameter validation.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Fischler and Bolles, Random Sample Consensus (1981)|https://doi.org/10.1145/358669.358692]

